::GEO_U02_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which rule translates a point 3 units right and 2 units down? {
= (x, y) → (x + 3, y - 2)#Correct. Moving right increases x by 3, and moving down decreases y by 2.
~ (x, y) → (x - 3, y + 2)#This reverses both directions. Left decreases x and up increases y.
~ (x, y) → (x + 2, y - 3)#This switches the horizontal and vertical changes.
~ (x, y) → (-x, -y)#This is a reflection through the origin or a 180° rotation, not a translation.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Point A(4, -5) is reflected across the x-axis. What are the coordinates of A'? {
~ (-4, -5)#This changes the x-coordinate, which matches reflection across the y-axis.
= (4, 5)#Correct. Reflection across the x-axis keeps x and changes the sign of y.
~ (-4, 5)#This changes both coordinate signs, which matches reflection through the origin.
~ (5, 4)#This switches the coordinates instead of reflecting across the x-axis.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which coordinate rule represents a reflection across the y-axis? {
~ (x, y) → (x, -y)#This is reflection across the x-axis because only y changes sign.
~ (x, y) → (-x, -y)#This changes both signs, which matches reflection through the origin or 180° rotation.
= (x, y) → (-x, y)#Correct. Reflection across the y-axis changes the sign of x only.
~ (x, y) → (y, x)#This switches coordinates and does not represent reflection across the y-axis.
}

::GEO_U02_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Point B(2, 7) is rotated 90° counterclockwise about the origin. What are the coordinates of B'? {
~ (7, -2)#This is the rule for a 90° clockwise rotation.
~ (-2, -7)#This does not match the 90° counterclockwise rotation rule.
~ (-7, -2)#This changes signs incorrectly for this rotation.
= (-7, 2)#Correct. A 90° counterclockwise rotation uses (x, y) → (-y, x).
}

::GEO_U02_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L01_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Point C(1, 2) maps to C'(-1, 2). Which transformation is shown? {
~ Reflection across the x-axis#Reflection across the x-axis would keep x and change y to -2.
~ Translation 2 units right#A translation 2 units right would map C to (3, 2), not (-1, 2).
= Reflection across the y-axis#Correct. The x-coordinate changes sign while the y-coordinate stays the same.
~ Rotation 90° clockwise#A 90° clockwise rotation would map (1, 2) to (2, -1).
}
